// The JVM symbol table shared by java.dl and kotlin.dl: what package each file
// declares, what top-level symbols it declares, and the (package, symbol)
// splits each of its imports asks for. Both languages fill all four relations,
// which is what makes Kotlin -> Java links fall out of the same two rules at no
// extra cost (on the reference monorepo every cross-language JVM edge runs into
// lib/java).
//
// Neither language has a per-module manifest in scope (no build.gradle; the one
// pom.xml is a third-party pin list owning no sources), so the JVM contributes
// no module_node — all of its value is in file_link, and its files fall into
// modules.dl's first-path-segment fallback.

.printsize
.decl jvm_package(file: string, pkg: string)
.decl jvm_decl(file: string, pkg: string, symbol: string)
.decl jvm_import(file: string, pkg: string, symbol: string)
.decl jvm_import_star(file: string, pkg: string)

.rule
// Resolve by DECLARED SYMBOL, not by filename and not by path. A JVM package is
// not a directory (41% of the .kt files on the reference repo sit outside
// src/main/kotlin and their package genuinely disagrees with their path), and
// the imported name matches the target's basename only about half the time
// (several public types per file). This is a two-column equality join, so no
// builtin runs inside it.
file_link(F, D) :- jvm_import(F, P, S), jvm_decl(D, P, S), F != D.
// `import a.b.*` names a package, not a file, so no single target is derivable —
// fan out to every file declaring that package. Deliberately NOT used as a
// fallback for ordinary imports: measured, that turns ~8.6k imports into ~128k
// file edges (~15x, one package here is declared by 94 files) and makes the SPA
// unusable. Star imports are rare enough that this fan-out stays bounded.
file_link(F, D) :- jvm_import_star(F, P), jvm_package(D, P), F != D.
